A||||28||loafer87gt||Failure to dispose of body properly? WTF?||||1||
Z||000000||loafer87gt||06-11-2001||03:19 PM||||There is a lady in Alberta, Rie Fuji, who has been charged with "failure to dispose of a body properly" after neighbours complained about a stench coming from her apartment building. Aparently neighbours heard the cries and screams of her 15 month old son for two full days before they ended, yet no one did anything. When the landlord finally did go check out the smell, they found the body of her dead son. Now they are searching for her toddler daughter, who they believe has been wrapped in plastic bags and set afloat in the river. What the hell is up with this "failure to dispose" charge? From my point of view, it seems like she should be charged with murder or manslaughter. They even found her diary that described the days leading up to her sons death and how she hated being a mother as she had no social life anymore, and was always stuck looking after the kids. What a sick sick world we live in, this sort of stuff just makes my stomach turn.<BR> <A HREF="http://cbc.ca/cgi-bin/templates/view.cgi?category=Canada&story=/news/2001/06/07/calgary_infant010607" TARGET=_blank>http://cbc.ca/cgi-bin/templates/view.cgi?category=Canada&story=/news/2001/06/07/calgary_infant010607</A> ||||reg||1
Z||000001||Ken Wittlief||06-11-2001||03:27 PM||||maybe that was the easiest thing they could come up with to arrest her for something, and they will put together their case and charge her with more later.<P>  the first step is to get her behind bars for something, so she cant flee, then to make the bigger charges stick.||||reg||1
