Z||000017||JSocha||06-12-2001||10:02 AM||||First of all, they would need to take "Cruel and Unusual Punishment" off the law books which is for the criminals protection.  Then all of the above suggestions/recommendations could have legally been performed.<P>Second, they should have tortured him...let him recover...shoved him in a dark, dark, dark cell with no human contact (and follow the scenarios like what they did in the movie "Murder in the First" about the prisoner at Alcatraz who tried to escape).<P>Third, after about 5 to 10 years of this, they should have set him free only after selling "licences" or "permits" to hunt him down with the stipulation that for the first 3 years after his release, no one could directly kill him, however, could maime him in any way possible to cause him extreme grief and pain like the animal he was.<P>Fourth, upon the third year where it would now be allowed to "legally" kill him (without reprocussions to the licenced individual), they should televise it nationally of him being finally tracked, stalked and killed (by any methods the individual killing him wants to use, either quick and/or long and/or slow) with the final message "This could happen to you!  Think before you do the unspeakable!" to get the message across to other potential criminals.<P>------------------<BR>     <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/jsocha_2.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000018||Justin||06-12-2001||10:04 AM||||The truly sad thing is that he didn't mind dieing, and that he said he won, his ultimate plan or whatever.  What I mean is, he wasn't scared to die, he wanted to, and he "defeated" the government in his head.  So we gave him what he wanted.  We should have put him in jail, for life, with the most cheap disgusting foods, no showers, no nothing.  Just a wall to stare at.  No work, that would give him something to do.  And then he would go insane and probably die a painful death from his living habits.  Then we'll see who "won".<P>------------------<BR>If at first you don't succeed, pretend you were never trying in the first place.<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.plauder-smilies.de/rough/greenchainsaw.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000019||Ken Wittlief||06-12-2001||10:50 AM||||I certainly understand the feelings and emotions behind the death penalty. this thread sure has opened up a can of worms.<P>There was a really great line in a really terrible movie (Lost in Space) when John first got his hands on Dr. Smith and wanted to kill him: <P>"You cant kill the monster, without becoming the monster".<P>As long as we, as a society, can rationalize the conditions and circumstances, under which it is acceptable, or necessary, to kill another human being, then individuals, like McVeigh, will do the same.<P> In his mind he was completely 'justified' in blowing up that building. We know this for a fact because he never repented or apologized.<P>  And in a sad way, by executing him, we verified at least part of his mindset, that "sometimes its necessary to murder people".<P>  the question is then left up to the individual, when is it necessary, when is it justified?<P>  We could have put an end to McVeighs influence and terror, if we had locked him up for life, with the public declaration that anyone who would kill another human is insane by default, mentally deficent, defective, and must be isolated from the normal people. Keep him sedated, keep him drugged, leave him on the edge of a coma most of the time, but treat him as defective, not evil.<P>  But then we would have to get rid of the armed police forces, and the military. We have a long way to go before we can do that.<P>  Yesterday was a grim reminder that, inspite of our civilazation, we are still a savage species, whether we like it or not.<P>  Yes it says in that book, an eye for an eye, and whoever commits murder shall be put to death. This gives us the legal and moral justification to put certain people to death.<P>  but if you page back towards the end of the book, you find the words attributed to God: "I seek mercy, not justice".<P>   When an arab teenage with a car bomb feels he has every right to blow up a school bus full of children - when he truely believes those children deserve to die, and his mission is a 'Just Cause', but instead he has mercy on them and lets them live<P>   when someone like McVeigh feels he has ever right to violently attack the government, but instead he has mercy on his 'enemies'<P>   when we are legally justified to execute a prisoner, but instead we have mercy on them<P>   when we set the example, when we set the tone, when we establish a new mindset for civilization, then we will be on our way. We have a long way to go.||||reg||1
Z||000020||SteveJ||06-12-2001||10:57 AM||||Punishment isn't always about the criminal. Sometimes its about setting an example. Those in the audience who would do such a thing now know they will pay with their life.||||reg||1
Z||000021||Ken Wittlief||06-12-2001||11:26 AM||||The problem is, there are plenty of people out there that are HAPPY to pay with their life.<P> How do you punish a suicide bomber?!<P>  an example is when you show someone how things are SUPPOSE to be done, not when you do the same thing to them they already did to someone else.<P>   thats just getting even.<P>  Im afraid the only example we made yesterday was VERIFYING to everyone that sometimes it is necessary to murder people. yesterdays execution did not end the cycle.<p>[This message has been edited by Ken Wittlief (edited 06-12-2001).]||||reg||1
